@prefix sh:      <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix orexis:      <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix sosa:    <http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/> .
@prefix sensing: <http://example.org/orexis/sensing#> .

#  THE ACTIONS THIS PACKAGE BRINGS — one node each, and the whole of one (knowledge/domain/action.md):
#    orexis:available  its PRECONDITION — a SELECT over the world binding ?property ?via ?direction,
#                  and ?for_agent where the row is a duty owed to someone; `$me` and
#                  `$properties` substituted by the collector, prefixes prepended by the store;
#    sh:construct  its EFFECT, with orexis:retracts, orexis:landsAfter and orexis:confirmedBy beside it;
#    (who carries it out is the code's to say: `@contributes(<this action>)` on its module.)
#  Precondition, effect and taker were three files and a fourth surface; a STRIPS operator is one
#  thing, and an author adds a way of acting by adding a node here and a `take()` to a module.

#################  What LOOKING makes true  #################
#
#  The one effect every agent has, and the one whose limits are worth stating carefully.
#
#  Looking does not change the world. What it changes is what the agent KNOWS, so this rule
#  predicts a reading with a current `sosa:resultTime` and the SAME VALUE the last one carried.
#  That is the whole of Observe's effect, and getting it wrong in the tempting direction — a
#  predicted reading sitting inside the region, since after all that is what the agent wants —
#  would teach a planner that a thirsty plant can be watered by looking at it.
#
#  A property nothing has ever read carries no value forward, and the construct then emits the
#  observation WITHOUT `sosa:hasSimpleResult`: SPARQL drops a triple whose terms are unbound, so
#  the omission needs no branch. That is honest — nobody can predict what a first look will say
#  — and it has a consequence a planner must handle rather than inherit: a valueless reading
#  satisfies "no reading sits below the floor" VACUOUSLY, so a region goal read as a whole would
#  look repaired by looking. The freshness half genuinely is; the range half is not. Judging
#  "goal achieved" is the planner's job (#239) and that is where the distinction has to live.
#
#  The node is NOT minted here. `sensed_writer` mints it from a Python slug of the feature and
#  the property, and re-deriving that in SPARQL would put the identity of an observation in two
#  places. The retraction FINDS the node by its pattern, and the construct reuses whichever it
#  found or a fresh blank node — which costs nothing, because no shape names an observation: they
#  ask whether one EXISTS with a property and a value, and a blank node exists exactly as well.

sensing:Observing
    a orexis:Action ;

    #  AVAILABLE where I may ASK an instrument that monitors my subject for this property —
    #  `sensing:mayAsk`, not `sensing:polls`, so a device that takes no orders offers no row
    #  (a-lever-an-agent-cannot-pull-is-not-a-lever). DISTINCT because a region carries three
    #  property shapes and all three are wants. Reads the whole default graph: wiring is public.
    orexis:available """
        SELECT DISTINCT ?want ?about ?via ?direction WHERE {
          VALUES (?want ?about) { $wants }
          $me sensing:mayAsk ?via .
          ?via sensing:monitors ?subject ; sosa:observes ?property .
          # A want ABOUT AN INSTRUMENT — knowing, the freshness want — is looked for through
          # exactly that instrument, whatever it watches; a want ABOUT A PROPERTY — a stake —
          # through any instrument of this agent's that reads it on the subject it acts for.
          FILTER(?via = ?about
                 || (?property = ?about && EXISTS { $me orexis:actsFor ?subject }))
        }""" ;

    rdfs:label "what looking makes true" ;

    #  WHO MADE IT is part of what a look makes true, and leaving it out was what let a
    #  DOSE satisfy an epistemic want. Every real observation carries `sosa:madeBySensor` —
    #  `sensed_writer` writes it unconditionally — and the freshness want asks for one made
    #  by the instrument it is about, so the two halves have to agree: a rule that reuses the
    #  stored node retracts all of its triples first, so a predicted look that did not
    #  re-state the sensor would come back having ERASED it, and the want would read unmet in
    #  the world its own repair reached.
    #
    #  It is also what tells the two kinds of prediction apart. A dose predicts a value and
    #  says nothing about who saw it, because nobody has: watering a plant does not produce a
    #  reading. Without this line the market's Acquire — whose effect also mints an
    #  observation, since predicting a value is the only way to state a change — satisfied
    #  "something read this recently" exactly as well as looking did, and satisfied it FIRST,
    #  `Acquire` sorting before `Observe`. A thirsty plant would have answered a stale
    #  reading by buying water.
    #
    #  The sensor is joined rather than passed: a rule's arguments are the want's, and which
    #  instrument serves a (subject, property) is the wiring's to say. `sensing:mayAsk` and
    #  not `sensing:polls`, so a device that takes no orders predicts nothing — the same
    #  fact the affordance row is built on, stated where a rule can see it. Two probes on one
    #  pair would bind twice and predict two readings; that wiring is a warning at validation
    #  already (one-agent-many-sensors) and no shipped world has it.
    sh:construct """
        CONSTRUCT {
            ?obs a sosa:Observation , ?band ;
                 sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest $subject ;
                 sosa:observedProperty ?property ;
                 sosa:madeBySensor ?sensor ;
                 sosa:resultTime ?now ;
                 sosa:hasSimpleResult ?value .
        } WHERE {
            $me sensing:mayAsk ?sensor .
            ?sensor sensing:monitors $subject ;
                    sosa:observes ?property .
            FILTER(?sensor = $about || ?property = $about)
            OPTIONAL { GRAPH $state {
                ?seen sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest $subject ;
                      sosa:observedProperty ?property .
                OPTIONAL { ?seen sosa:hasSimpleResult ?value }
                #  And what the reading IS, carried through (#579): a look changes what you
                #  know, never the band the reading is in, and a predicted reading has no
                #  number to be classified from again.
                OPTIONAL { ?seen a ?band . FILTER(?band != sosa:Observation) }
            } }
            BIND(COALESCE(?seen, BNODE()) AS ?obs)
            #  STAMPED WHEN THIS ACT'S CHANGE COMPLETES (#588), not when the plan was made:
            #  a reading this construct predicts exists at the instant the act lands, and
            #  `$lands` is that instant — the node's own plus this act's `orexis:landsAfter`.
            BIND($lands AS ?now)
        }""" ;

    #  ZERO, and it is not a shrug. Looking changes NOTHING about the world — the pot is exactly
    #  as wet after the reading as before it — so the world-change completes instantly and
    #  emptily. What a look delays is KNOWLEDGE, and that is what the route below says. The
    #  record's sentence, from the other side: a sensing action changes what you know, not what
    #  is.
    orexis:landsAfter """
        SELECT (0 AS ?seconds) WHERE { }""" ;

    #  The reading arriving IS the confirmation, which makes this the one act whose landing and
    #  whose confirmation are the same event.

    orexis:retracts """
        CONSTRUCT { ?obs ?p ?o } WHERE {
            ?sensor sensing:monitors $subject ; sosa:observes ?property .
            FILTER(?sensor = $about || ?property = $about)
            GRAPH $state {
                ?obs sosa:hasFeatureOfInterest $subject ;
                     sosa:observedProperty ?property ;
                     ?p ?o
            }
        }""" .
